              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Grid Transformation**\n\nI'm focusing on ARC grid transformation. I've realized the core issue is going from a 3x3 to a 9x9 grid. Fractals or Kronecker products seemed promising initially, but example 3 changed my perspective. The output appears as blocks. It's now evident that the process seems to involve the replication and, perhaps, manipulation of the input's elements into a larger structure.\n\n\n**Uncovering Macro Patterns**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. I noticed the key to example 3 lies in the inverse relationship between the input and the blocks in the output, along with an emergent macro pattern. Specifically, I've confirmed that each block can be derived through an inverse transformation. This macro pattern itself mirrors the inverse of the original input grid, replicating this relationship across the larger grid.\n\n\n**Confirming Inverses' Role**\n\nI've made a crucial verification. Examining the structure of blocks in a 9x9 grid, I noticed a strong correlation with the inverse of the initial input. The blocks within the output grid appear to correspond directly to the inverse transformation of the input grid, with zeroed blocks linked to zeros in a macro grid. This pattern holds true across various examples, solidifying my understanding of the transformation.\n\n\n",
